A||||11||MartyA||Transaxle/Clutch
Z||000000||MartyA||06-23-99||07:51 PM||||I have a 1985 Fiero V6 with the 4 speed manual transaxle.  About a month ago the car failed to go into any gear yet the clutch felt reasonably normal.  (The car also would not start until I jumped the electrical clutch safety feature.  It now starts fine but still will not go into any gear).  I've begun the joyous process of removing the transaxle, hopefully to find something broken.  Is this the right move or should I be looking for something else in the system, e.g. the hydraulics?
Z||000001||PaUL||06-23-99||08:05 PM||||The most common cause of this is the clutch slave cyclinder. Read the earlier post called Clutch Info Uncovered for more details.
Z||000002||mwbackus||06-24-99||01:10 AM||||1.  Check to see if you have a bent clutch pedal, the height of the clutch pedal should exceed that of the brake pedal by about 1".  The aluminum clutch pedals are well known to bend and a steel replacement is available.<p>2.   Check the clutch lever on the trans.  This lever (at the end of the slave pushrod) tends to crack where it attaches to the clutch fork shaft.  This causes it to slip and results in a clutch that "feels" normal but does not disengage.  This aluminum lever was also replaced with a cast steel version in later years, and the newer version is readily available.
Z||000003||ChadMan||06-24-99||07:18 AM||||If your clutch pedal is in fact bent, V8 Archie has a really good indepth discussion on how to replace it at www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm.  This involves replacing the pedal with a GM replacement part #10066423.  There's also a little bit of info toward the end of the "Clutch Info Uncovered..." post.<p>What I'm wondering is, why couldn't a fellow just get a pedal out of a junked 87 or 88 Fiero, which had the improved pedal from the factory?  I'm probably missing something.
Z||000004||Carrolles||06-24-99||11:15 AM||||Will it shift into gear if the engine is not running?
Z||000005||MartyA||06-24-99||05:55 PM||||The clutch seems to be disengaged at all times.  The car will not go into gear even when the car is not running.  The shifter seems to be working and the stick actually feels like you have moved it into a gear but it is not.  With the car off, it still rolls freely even after you shift the stick into "what appears to be" a gear.  And with the car running, it is the same thing.  There also is no grind of any kind when you move the shifter through any/all the gears, (again with the car running and the clutch in or out).  It seems that if the slave was the problem the clutch would remain engaged in which case if you shifted with the car running there would be a transmission grind.  Aren't there some forks within the transaxle which, if broken, would result in the inability to engage the clutch??
Z||000006||Lynx||06-25-99||01:50 AM||||Check your shift cables FIRST. I broke the plastic housing for a shift cable and it would just the the cable bind instead of going into gear. It sounds like its just the shifter. Go to the tranny and try to use the levers on the trans to shift it. Check all this stuff, pull the inside consule and look at the cables and then check them in the engine area. 
Z||000007||mwbackus||06-25-99||04:22 PM||||I would have to agree with that.  If there were a malfunction in the clutch system it would be engaged all the time.  It is therefore a shift linkage problem.  Easy to tell if someone observes the shift levers on the trans as you move through the gears.  Could just be the keeper on one of the cables has come off or broke. 
Z||000008||Bruce||06-27-99||08:09 PM||||I'm having a little trouble shifting from 2nd to 3rd- it gets there, but I have to work at it. The clutch; slave and master cylinders were replaced just a few months ago. I'm hoping that it's just an adjustment needed at the cables. Any suggestions?
Z||000009||Carrolles||06-28-99||11:57 AM||||Concerning the trouble shifting from 2nd to 3rd:  If this is an 85 4spd then it is the Muncie transmission.  I had the same trouble and finally fixed it.  Where the shift cables end at the transmission there is a lever with a pin in it that raises and lowers the shift shaft in the trans case.  This pin rides in a slot in the shift shaft and eventually wears flat. The resulting slop in the linkage causes hard shifting in 3rd/4th.  The part is no longer available but I found one through a chevy dealer in Washington state.  There is another fix.  The pin is pressed into the stamped steel lever arm.  You can lock the pin in a vice and rotate in 120 degrees.  This places the flattened side out of the way and restores clearences.  If you rotate it 180 degrees you wear the side opposite of the flattened side thus giving you only one more use from the part.  Rotating it 120 degrees allows you to later rotate it 120 degrees again and get an additional use out of the part.<p>I have the part number at home.  If anyone needs it I'll look it up when I go home this weekend.<p>Incidentally this also eventually happens to the Muncie/Getrag 5spd.  Rodney Dickman makes replacement parts for this trans that are superior to the original.  He does not make the part for the Muncie 4spd.<p>Hope this helps,<p>Carroll
Z||000010||Bruce||06-30-99||05:57 PM||||That's what it was. I "suggested" it to the mechanic (didn't want to insult his intelligence), and it shifts 100% better.<br>Now, on to the radio..............
Z||000011||Bruce||06-30-99||08:11 PM||||Is the clutch pedal change (from stock to steel) still a good move, no matter what adjustments are made?
